// Unsafe selfdestruct: a `SELFDESTRUCT` opcode that executed
// successfully within the trace.
//
// Why this is the right structural signature, and what it cannot
// claim: `SELFDESTRUCT` irrevocably removes a contract's code and
// sweeps its entire remaining balance to a beneficiary address
// (`fact_model::CallKind::Selfdestruct`, `Call.to`). It is one of the
// few EVM operations whose mere occurrence in an observed trace is
// itself significant to a reviewer, independent of who triggered it —
// unlike a plain value-transferring `CALL`, there is no benign,
// routine reason for it to appear at all in most contracts' normal
// operation. SWC-106 / SCWE-050 describe the underlying weakness as
// this instruction being reachable *without* proper access control;
// this pattern cannot verify authorization directly (see the scope
// note below), but a `SELFDESTRUCT` that reached execution and
// succeeded is exactly the kind of candidate a reviewer needs
// surfaced to check whether that access control existed.
//
// Both the fact (`fact_model::CallKind::Selfdestruct`) and the
// predicate support (`call(kind: SelfDestruct, ...)`) are new in this
// pattern's own milestone — see `crates/fact-model/src/call.rs`,
// `crates/dsl/src/schema.rs`, and `crates/matcher/src/predicate.rs`'s
// `call_kind_matches`. No other file needed to change: `succeeded`
// already existed on `call(...)` (added for `unchecked_external_call`),
// and grounding verifies this evidence the same generic way it
// verifies every other `call(...)` clause.
//
// Scope note: this pattern flags "a SELFDESTRUCT call succeeded" as a
// reviewable candidate. It cannot (and does not claim to) determine
// whether the call was actually authorized — the fact model has no
// access-control/ownership fact (see the top-level README's "Access-
// control failure" row in the "Exploit families evaluated but not
// added" table for the same, still-unresolved gap). A contract whose
// `SELFDESTRUCT` is correctly gated behind an `onlyOwner`-style check,
// destroyed by its legitimate owner, will still match; this is a
// candidate for review, not a proof of missing access control.
pattern unsafe_selfdestruct version 1 {
    family: UnsafeSelfdestruct
    severity: Critical
    tags: ["selfdestruct", "access-control", "irrevocable"]
    references: ["SWC-106", "SCWE-050"]

    evidence {
        required destruct_call: call(kind: SelfDestruct, succeeded: true)
    }

    constraint: destruct_call
}
